This changes the picture significantly. NSS=Flags=internal,moduleDBOnly,critical is a module-level descriptor, not a slot cipher configuration line. They are different record types in the same file format.

What this line actually means:

    internal — this module is internal to NSS; it cannot be substituted by a filesystem module with the same name. This is a security-positive flag that prevents module hijacking. Keep it.
    moduleDBOnly — this record functions solely as the module database pointer. It does not expose crypto slots. It tells NSS where to find other modules. Removing it breaks module loading entirely.
    critical — if this record fails to load, NSS initialization fails hard rather than silently continuing without it. Hard failure on missing security infrastructure is correct behavior. Keep it.

There is nothing to harden on this line. All three flags are correct and should be left exactly as-is.

