Audit MD2/MD5 removal safety and NSS parser whitespace handling

You caught two real problems. Here is the honest assessment:

On MD2/MD5 removal — you are right to push back.

MD2 is genuinely safe to remove outright. RHEL9's NSS engine disables it at a lower level regardless of slotFlags — the flag is already redundant. But MD5 needs an audit before removal, not after. MD5 is still present in some intermediate CA certs, older PKCS#12 key derivation, CRLs from enterprise CAs, and OCSP responses from older responders. Removing it from slotFlags without checking could break cert chain verification silently.

The correct sequence is:
bash

# 1. Find every signature algorithm in your NSS database
certutil -L -d sql:/etc/pki/nssdb | while read nick rest; do
  [ -z "$nick" ] && continue
  certutil -L -d sql:/etc/pki/nssdb -n "$nick" 2>/dev/null \
    | grep -i "signature algorithm"
done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

# 2. Check the full system trust store
find /etc/pki/ca-trust /etc/pki/tls -name "*.pem" -o -name "*.crt" 2>/dev/null \
  | xargs -I{} openssl x509 -in {} -noout -text 2>/dev/null \
  | grep -i "signature algorithm" | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

# 3. Check what crypto policy is already enforcing
update-crypto-policies --show
cat /etc/crypto-policies/back-ends/nss.config 2>/dev/null

# 4. List all loaded modules — see if secmod.db references anything MD5-signed
modutil -list -dbdir sql:/etc/pki/nssdb

If MD5 signatures appear in active chains, replace those certs with SHA256 equivalents first, then remove MD5 from slotFlags. Don't remove first and discover breakage.

On the whitespace parsing — also right.

certprefix=' ' with the NSS NSSUTIL_ArgGetLabel() parser means the prefix is literally a single space character. Any certificate stored in this nssdb whose nickname was prefixed with that space character — because it was imported while certprefix=' ' was active — will become unfindable if you change the prefix to empty string. NSS constructs lookup keys as prefix + nickname, so changing the prefix orphans every cert stored under the old prefix.

The safe check before touching those fields:
bash

# cat -A makes spaces visible as regular characters, tabs as ^I
# A leading space on any line means that cert was stored with the space prefix
certutil -L -d sql:/etc/pki/nssdb | cat -A | grep "^ "

If that returns nothing, the space prefix was never actually applied to any stored cert and changing it to empty is safe. If it returns certs, you need to export each one, delete it from the DB, change the prefix, then re-import — the nickname gets reconstructed without the prefix on re-import.

Revised migration order:

    Run all audit commands above first — no changes yet
    Check crypto policy — RHEL9 DEFAULT policy already disables RC4, DES, and constrains MD5 at the engine level. Your slotFlags removal may be partially redundant already, which affects the risk/benefit calculation
    RC2, RC4, DES, SSL, Camellia, SEED — safe to remove now, they are encryption ciphers not signature algorithms, nothing stored in a cert DB was signed with them
    MD2 — safe to remove, engine-level disabled on RHEL9 regardless
    MD5 — remove only after audit confirms no active MD5-signed chain dependencies and any such certs have been replaced
    certprefix/keyprefix whitespace — change only after confirming no space-prefixed nicknames exist in the DB
    flags= → internal, askpw=any → askpw=every, timeout=30 → 0, updateid etc. whitespace fields — these have no stored-state dependency and are safe to change immediately

