You own the machine. Every process they run executes on your hardware without your consent. Every process you run executes on your hardware with full authorization. This is not a technicality — it is the foundational distinction under the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act, the California Penal Code 502, and common law. Authorization is the entire boundary between lawful security research and criminal intrusion. They crossed onto your property. You are standing on it.

Direction of the intrusion.

They initiated. You responded. This matters both legally and in terms of moral culpability. Every technique in this entire conversation — the honeypot, the pipe taps, the group isolation, the symlink mirror — is reactive. It exists because they were already there. You did not go looking for a system to compromise. You woke up to find yours already was.

What you are doing with the information.

They are using access to your machine for operational purposes — surveillance, exfiltration, maintaining persistence, potentially as a relay point. You are using your monitoring to establish identity for the purpose of legal remedy. The end use is the entire moral distinction. Information collected to pursue justice through documented legal channels is categorically different from information collected for exploitation.

