The framework you are thinking of is the Essential Eight from the Australian Cyber Security Centre (ACSC). RHEL implements it via a combination of SELinux, audit, PAM, and crypto policies. Let me build the full structure:
Ran a command, created a file, read a file

Here is the complete structure and what each layer maps to:

The Essential Eight in this context:

Controls 1 through 8 are all implemented, but the most important for your threat model are 5, 6, and 7. Control 5 restricts admin privileges — PAM lockout after 5 attempts, 15-minute unlock, 15-character minimum passwords matching ACSC Maturity Level 3. Control 6 is the full sysctl RHEL STIG baseline — every network hardening parameter RHEL ships with, plus the ones RHEL omits. Control 7 enforces SSH key-only authentication with no password fallback — the single most impactful change you can make to a network device.

The RHEL structural equivalents:

RHEL's /etc/crypto-policies/ becomes /etc/ssl/ctw-crypto-policy.conf. RHEL's auditd rules become /etc/audit/ctw-audit.rules applied at boot. RHEL's PAM stack maps directly since OpenWrt supports PAM. RHEL's modprobe.d blacklist is applied in rc.local. RHEL's SELinux confinement is approximated via Yama ptrace scope 2 plus kernel lockdown in confidentiality mode.

Two sysctl entries worth highlighting specifically:

vm.unprivileged_userfaultfd = 0 — mirrors what RHEL9.2 added after userfaultfd became the dominant privilege escalation primitive. kernel.io_uring_disabled = 2 — RHEL9 restricted this in its latest releases because io_uring has become the new BPF in terms of exploit primitives. Both are set here.

To build with all of this:
bash

# Run the script from imagebuilder root
bash ctw_essential8_openwrt.sh

# Then build with the files overlay
make image PROFILE=prpl_haze PACKAGES="" FILES=files/ V=s

The FILES=files/ argument to make is what tells imagebuilder to overlay everything we wrote into the final image before packaging the ITB.
